finding.reasoning:
Confirmed in the real app. Every generated report (bid defense summary, country recommendation, site engagement pack) and the run-manifest JSON says there has been no retrospective validation against recruitment outcomes. That is factually wrong after v0.9.3 and v0.9.4, which back-tested 57 completed trials and recalibrated σ and comparator matching on them. The README already says so, and the bid summary's own risk text cites those back-tests. Who it matters for: sponsors, CRO feasibility leads and auditors who read the exported reports. A self-contradicting or inaccurate limitations statement weakens trust in the documents and misstates the evidence. It does not change any number or decision, so severity is low.

The opposite mistake is just as important to avoid. The back-test is not independent validation: the same 57 trials were used to tune the calibration (σ rule, matching thresholds), the final site counts flatter the patients-per-site matching, and only one true hold-out exists (KEYNOTE-407). So the replacement wording does three things:
- names the back-test and its headline figures, tied to v0.9.4 defaults;
- says the trials were also used for calibration;
- says it is exploratory and not prospective or held-out predictive validation.

